From: Maximilian Gaedig <sly-keg-cheating@duck.com>
Subject: [PATCH] scsi: ufs: qcom: fix lane clock refcount leak on resume

ufs_qcom_disable_lane_clks() is guarded by host->is_lane_clks_enabled, but
ufs_qcom_enable_lane_clks() is not. The resume path enables the lane clocks
twice -- once from ufs_qcom_setup_clocks() on POST_CHANGE and again from
ufs_qcom_resume() -- while suspend disables them once, and because the
disable side is guarded it cannot compensate. Every runtime suspend/resume
cycle therefore leaks one reference on each lane clock.

Measured on a OnePlus 6 (sdm845) over four forced cycles:

  clk_bulk_enable   8   <- ufs_qcom_enable_lane_clks   (twice per cycle)
  clk_bulk_disable  4   <- ufs_qcom_suspend            (once per cycle)
  gcc_ufs_phy_ice_core_clk  +4 over the same four cycles

The references accumulate without bound (observed reaching 256 over an hour of
uptime) and, because these clocks are parented to gpll0 and thence to the
rpmhcc XO, they keep bi_tcxo enabled. clk-rpmh then writes xo.lvl = 0x3 ("on")
into the RPMH sleep set, so AOSS can never reach its deep states and qcom_stats
aosd and cxsd stay pinned at 0.

Mirror the guard from the disable path. After this change the refcount drains
to 0 when idle and the sleep set reads cx.lvl = mx.lvl = xo.lvl = 0.

Signed-off-by: Maximilian Gaedig <sly-keg-cheating@duck.com>
---
--- a/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c	2026-09-03 01:24:06.471687145 +0200
+++ b/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c	2026-09-03 01:24:06.504516634 +0200
@@ -356,6 +356,17 @@
 {
 	int err;
 
+	/*
+	 * Mirror the guard in ufs_qcom_disable_lane_clks(). The resume path
+	 * enables the lane clocks twice -- once from ufs_qcom_setup_clocks()
+	 * on POST_CHANGE and again from ufs_qcom_resume() -- while suspend
+	 * disables them once, and the disable side is guarded so it cannot
+	 * compensate. Without this check each runtime suspend/resume cycle
+	 * leaked one reference on every lane clock.
+	 */
+	if (host->is_lane_clks_enabled)
+		return 0;
+
 	err = clk_bulk_prepare_enable(host->num_clks, host->clks);
 	if (err)
 		return err;
